A 2D table keeps track of every sum that can be made from the array. After filling it, the reachable sum closest to half of the total gives the smallest difference.

The array values are assumed to be non-negative. This version uses more memory, but the state is easy to follow.

```cpp
class Solution{

  public:
    int minDifference(vector<int>& arr, int n)  {
        int total = 0;
        for (int value : arr) {
            total += value;
        }

        int limit = total / 2;
        vector<vector<bool>> possible(n + 1, vector<bool>(limit + 1, false));

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            possible[i][0] = true;
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int current = 1; current <= limit; current++) {
                possible[i][current] = possible[i - 1][current];

                if (arr[i - 1] <= current) {
                    possible[i][current] = possible[i][current] ||
                                           possible[i - 1][current - arr[i - 1]];
                }
            }
        }

        for (int sum1 = limit; sum1 >= 0; sum1--) {
            if (possible[n][sum1]) {
                return total - 2 * sum1;
            }
        }

        return total;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * totalSum)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * totalSum)` in the worst case.

For example, with `[1, 6, 11, 5]`, a reachable sum of `11` is closest to half of `23`, so the answer is `23 - 2 * 11 = 1`.

An empty array or an array containing only zeros returns `0`.

A subset sum is enough here. Start with only sum `0` as reachable. For each value, copy the old reachable sums by adding the value. At the end, scan down from half of the total and use the first reachable sum.

The loop goes backward through the current sums. That makes sure one array element is not used more than once.

```python
class Solution:
    def minDifference(self, arr, n):
        total = sum(arr)
        target = total // 2
        reachable = [False] * (target + 1)
        reachable[0] = True

        for value in arr[:n]:
            for current in range(target, value - 1, -1):
                if reachable[current - value]:
                    reachable[current] = True

        for first_sum in range(target, -1, -1):
            if reachable[first_sum]:
                return total - 2 * first_sum

        return total
```

Time complexity: `O(n * totalSum)`.
Space complexity: `O(totalSum)`.

Dry run for `[1, 2, 7]`: the total is `10`, and the reachable sum nearest to `5` is `3`. The two sums become `3` and `7`, giving a difference of `4`.

The code assumes non-negative values, which is the usual form of this problem.

This uses a packed bit array instead of a full DP table. Bit `k` means that sum `k` can be formed. Adding a value shifts the bits, then ORs them with the old bits.

The code keeps the input unchanged. It is a little less obvious than normal DP, but it saves a lot of memory when the total sum is large.

```cpp
class Solution{

  public:
    int minDifference(vector<int>& arr, int n)  {
        int total = 0;
        for (int value : arr) {
            total += value;
        }

        int words = total / 64 + 1;
        vector<unsigned long long> bits(words, 0);
        bits[0] = 1ULL;

        for (int value : arr) {
            int wordShift = value / 64;
            int bitShift = value % 64;

            for (int word = words - 1; word >= wordShift; word--) {
                unsigned long long shifted =
                    bits[word - wordShift] << bitShift;

                if (bitShift != 0 && word - wordShift - 1 >= 0) {
                    shifted |= bits[word - wordShift - 1] >> (64 - bitShift);
                }

                bits[word] |= shifted;
            }
        }

        for (int sum1 = total / 2; sum1 >= 0; sum1--) {
            int word = sum1 / 64;
            int bit = sum1 % 64;

            if ((bits[word] & (1ULL << bit)) != 0) {
                return total - 2 * sum1;
            }
        }

        return total;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * totalSum / 64)`.
Space complexity: `O(totalSum / 64)`.

For an input such as `[0, 0, 4]`, zero values simply leave the reachable sums unchanged, and the result is `4`. The empty input also gives `0`.

The two subsets are decided one element at a time. The recursive function stores the best sum that can be made without going over half of the total. Taking that sum as `best`, the other subset has sum `total - best`, so the difference is `total - 2 * best`.

The memo table prevents solving the same index and remaining-sum pair again.

```java
class Solution {
    public int minDifference(int[] arr, int n) {
        int total = 0;
        for (int value : arr) {
            total += value;
        }

        int target = total / 2;
        int[][] memo = new int[n + 1][target + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int sum = 0; sum <= target; sum++) {
                memo[i][sum] = -1;
            }
        }

        int best = findBest(arr, n, target, memo);
        return total - 2 * best;
    }

    private int findBest(int[] arr, int count, int remaining, int[][] memo) {
        if (count == 0 || remaining == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[count][remaining] != -1) {
            return memo[count][remaining];
        }

        int skip = findBest(arr, count - 1, remaining, memo);
        int take = 0;

        if (arr[count - 1] <= remaining) {
            take = arr[count - 1] +
                   findBest(arr, count - 1, remaining - arr[count - 1], memo);
        }

        memo[count][remaining] = Math.max(skip, take);
        return memo[count][remaining];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * totalSum)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * totalSum)` for the memo table, plus recursion stack space.

This does not change the input array. If every item has the same value, the recursion still checks whether the values can be split evenly.

